I’ll inspect the diffraction data and metadata first, then identify candidate phases, refine them against the full pattern, and validate that all requested outputs are internally consistent. I’ll keep the phase count conservative and flag any ambiguity in the final report.

The chemistry points to the Mn–W–O system, but at 500 °C unreacted oxides and intermediate tungstates are all plausible. I’m now extracting peak positions and checking the installed phase-search/refinement utilities before committing to candidate structures.

The three strongest low-angle lines form the characteristic split cluster of monoclinic WO₃, while the strong features near 33–35° suggest at least one additional Mn-bearing phase. I’m comparing every locally available Mn–W–O, Mn oxide, and tungsten oxide reference rather than relying on those fingerprints alone.

I’ve narrowed the dominant phase to room-temperature monoclinic WO₃ (the P2₁/n/P2₁/c structural family); its split peak spacings match much better than tetragonal, orthorhombic, triclinic, MnWO₄, or Mn₃WO₆ references. I’m starting whole-pattern refinements now, then I’ll inspect residual peaks for any Mn oxide or unreacted component.

The first whole-pattern fit confirms that essentially every resolved line belongs to the WO₃ lattice; a one-phase model already reaches \(R_{wp}\approx11\%\). The main remaining mismatch is among overlapping 33–35° reflections, so I’m testing the closely related monoclinic/triclinic settings and better coordinate sets before deciding whether that residual is structural distortion, profile anisotropy, or a genuine minor phase.

The monoclinic model is the chemically and thermodynamically expected room-temperature form. A lower-symmetry P‑1 trial only improves \(R_{wp}\) by about 0.3 percentage point and refines back toward pseudo-monoclinic metrics, so I’m checking whether that small gain survives coordinate refinement and whether it is statistically/physically justified rather than overfitting peak asymmetry.

The residual test has revealed a chemically meaningful second fingerprint: peaks at about 33.27, 38.54, 49.65, 55.49, and 66.05° line up with bixbyite Mn₂O₃ after the same zero correction. MnWO₄ is ruled out by the absence of its intense 30.0/30.5° doublet, and Mn₃WO₆ by the missing ~20.5° line. I’m quantifying the WO₃ + Mn₂O₃ model and checking alternative Mn-oxide polymorphs now.

The two-phase refinement is now stable: triclinic/pseudo-monoclinic WO₃ plus cubic bixbyite Mn₂O₃ gives \(R_{wp}\approx7.85\%\), and the refined split is about 74.6/25.4 wt%. That ratio is also chemically consistent with a 2 WO₃ : Mn₂O₃ precursor mixture. I’m doing profile-model sensitivity checks because no calibrated instrument function was supplied; the formal GSAS-II fraction uncertainty alone would otherwise be too optimistic.

The phase fractions are stable within about 0.4 wt% across reasonable fixed profile functions. The larger uncertainty is crystallographic pseudosymmetry: WO₃ is only marginally triclinic, and Mn₂O₃ has nearly indistinguishable cubic/weakly orthorhombic descriptions at this peak breadth. I’m selecting the lowest-symmetry models only where the fit improvement is supported and will state that ambiguity explicitly.

The final calibration uses the reflection geometry explicitly: a refined specimen displacement (~−0.52 mm) fits slightly better than a constant zero offset and returns the Mn₂O₃ cell to its reference value. I’m now freezing that physically consistent profile, extending the calculation to every supplied point, and exporting the final project/CIF/profile set from one internally consistent GSAS-II state.

